<article class="env-lemma" id="01TI">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01TI">Lemma <span data-tag="01TI">29.57.2</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. The set of points of $X$ where $f$ is quasi-finite is an open $U \subset X$. The induced morphism $U \to S$ is locally quasi-finite. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose $f$ is quasi-finite at $x$. Let $x \in U = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \subset X$, $V = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \subset S$ be affine opens as in Definition <a href="/tag/01TD" data-tag="01TD">29.21.1</a>. By either Theorem <a href="/tag/03GT" data-tag="03GT">29.57.1</a> above or Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00QA" data-tag="00QA">10.123.13</a>, the set of primes $\mathfrak q$ at which $R \to A$ is quasi-finite is open in $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$. Since these all correspond to points of $X$ where $f$ is quasi-finite we get the first statement. The second statement is obvious. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>